{
 "voice": "Michael (Kokoro am_michael)",
 "lines": {
  "Take a slow breath in, and let it go.": "492cd214b870.mp3",
  "Welcome. Let's take a moment to slow down.": "547df4678fc0.mp3",
  "Now breathe in through your nose for four, and out slowly for six. Let the out-breath be longer.": "918326fc4c9d.mp3",
  "Let your breathing return to its own pace.": "1ad9a9530844.mp3",
  "Notice how you feel now, compared with a few minutes ago. Take that calm with you.": "f28de831a8d9.mp3",
  "Sit comfortably, and let your shoulders drop away from your ears.": "7d3cb2d05e02.mp3",
  "First, two double breaths. In through the nose, then a little more, then a long sigh out.": "965755dd1038.mp3",
  "If your mind wanders, that's fine. Just come back to the next breath.": "0b4962aa0564.mp3",
  "Let's steady the rhythm. In for four, hold for four, out for four, hold for four.": "8581fd7451a0.mp3",
  "And back to long, easy exhales.": "893f099dfb8e.mp3",
  "Today's theme: What's up to you.": "d26caee9a5d7.mp3",
  "The Stoic teacher Epictetus opened his handbook with one idea. Some things are up to us, and some are not. Our judgments, our effort and our choices are ours. Most everything else is not.": "abfcaf2adfd9.mp3",
  "Bring to mind something that's been weighing on you. Which part of it is actually up to you?": "b73dc1d5a590.mp3",
  "Today, put your effort where it counts, and let the rest be.": "aac69458c17d.mp3",
  "Welcome. Find a comfortable position, and close your eyes if you like.": "1ad70fd8a9ce.mp3",
  "Take a few slow breaths to arrive.": "e158209d1296.mp3",
  "Much of our stress comes from gripping things we can't control. Other people's opinions. The traffic. How a meeting will go. We spend our energy there, and have less left for what we can actually do.": "e9aa94549a70.mp3",
  "This isn't giving up. It's aiming. You put your full effort where it counts, and you let the outcome be what it will be.": "92f0e9eb52cc.mp3",
  "Now picture letting go of the rest, just for today. Notice how that feels in your body.": "5414c6607466.mp3",
  "Come back to your breath, and to the room around you.": "9eea02fc3564.mp3",
  "What is one small thing within your control that you can do about it today?": "09d84a5c4927.mp3",
  "Today's theme: Thoughts are not facts.": "ea617cce12a5.mp3",
  "Your mind is a busy storyteller. It predicts, judges and warns, all day long. Some of its stories are useful. Some are just noise, said with total confidence.": "ca3c6a2542bf.mp3",
  "Notice a thought that's been loud lately. Say it silently, with the words I'm having the thought that, in front of it.": "8af42fac8864.mp3",
  "Today, when a harsh thought shows up, name it as a thought, and keep going.": "d9d1001cc406.mp3",
  "Acceptance and commitment therapy has a simple move for this. Instead of thinking, I'm going to fail, you notice: I'm having the thought that I'm going to fail.": "aba63c992141.mp3",
  "The thought is still there. But now you're looking at it, rather than through it. That small gap gives you room to choose what to do next.": "5e19389edb59.mp3",
  "Watch how the thought sits there now. You don't have to argue with it, or obey it.": "a480daeafff3.mp3",
  "If that thought weren't steering, what would you choose to do today?": "99263a4741f5.mp3",
  "Today's theme: The obstacle is the way.": "d81bc29ab7ae.mp3",
  "Marcus Aurelius ran an empire through war and plague. In his private notebook he wrote that the obstacle to action can advance action. What stands in the way becomes the way.": "56d8e001ff60.mp3",
  "Think of an obstacle in your life right now. Hold it in mind without pushing it away.": "5e9e31706028.mp3",
  "Today, when something gets in your way, ask what it's giving you the chance to practice.": "988ea437d916.mp3",
  "A setback can be a chance to practice patience. A difficult person can be a chance to practice kindness. A failure can teach what success never would.": "3ac6d93f50a3.mp3",
  "This doesn't make hard things pleasant. It means no situation is wasted. There's always something in it you can use.": "b09a24166a0c.mp3",
  "Ask yourself: what quality could this help me build? Patience, courage, creativity?": "a00bdaf48190.mp3",
  "Picture yourself meeting it with that quality. What does the first step look like?": "a6281e19ee2b.mp3",
  "Today's theme: Leaves on a stream.": "db4969b88fb4.mp3",
  "Here is an exercise from acceptance and commitment therapy. Imagine sitting beside a gentle stream. Leaves float past on the water.": "74075c92b0be.mp3",
  "Picture the stream now. As each thought arrives, set it on a leaf, and let the water carry it.": "d46761f77aeb.mp3",
  "Today, when your mind gets busy, picture the stream, and let one thought float by.": "ebbd3808d974.mp3",
  "Each time a thought comes, place it on a leaf, and watch it drift away. Worries, plans, memories, even the thought that this is silly. All of them go on leaves.": "526eef704f39.mp3",
  "You're not trying to stop thinking. You're practicing letting thoughts come and go, without being swept downstream with them.": "cf915a8abb30.mp3",
  "If you get pulled into a thought, that's normal. Notice it, and gently return to the bank.": "dc7716ffd1cd.mp3",
  "Notice the space between thoughts, however brief. Rest there for a moment.": "1c083bafbe01.mp3",
  "Today's theme: It's the story, not the event.": "c574633898d2.mp3",
  "Epictetus taught that people are disturbed not by things, but by their opinions about things. The event happens. Then we add a story about what it means.": "e7d9dae707aa.mp3",
  "Recall something that annoyed or upset you recently. What actually happened, in plain facts?": "b738f3b9edaf.mp3",
  "Today, when something stings, separate what happened from the story you're telling about it.": "8e2791b6ebbf.mp3",
  "Someone is late, and the story becomes, they don't respect me. A plan falls through, and it becomes, nothing ever works out. The story often hurts more than the event.": "2e03a202a2de.mp3",
  "You can't always change what happens. But you can examine the story, and ask whether it's true, and whether it helps.": "aaf0d343a062.mp3",
  "Now notice the story you added on top. What did you tell yourself it meant?": "7d0dc9a4c4fa.mp3",
  "Is there another way to see it, one that's just as true, and kinder?": "940e92221405.mp3",
  "Today's theme: Making room.": "36df5ce02a64.mp3",
  "When a hard feeling shows up, our instinct is to fight it, or run from it. But pushing feelings away often makes them louder, and longer.": "7b542e5da6bf.mp3",
  "Notice any uncomfortable feeling present right now. Where do you feel it in your body?": "6f0b34956572.mp3",
  "Today, let difficult feelings come along for the ride, without letting them drive.": "de88ff4f1a50.mp3",
  "Acceptance and commitment therapy offers another option. Make room for the feeling. Let it be there, the way you'd let a guest sit in the corner while you get on with your day.": "625fe4b82599.mp3",
  "You don't have to like the feeling. You just stop spending all your energy wrestling with it.": "542de865daf9.mp3",
  "Breathe into that place. Imagine the space around it getting a little bigger, so the feeling has room.": "3531feb31d2c.mp3",
  "Say silently: I can feel this, and still do what matters to me.": "9417ae4f6567.mp3",
  "Today's theme: Life is long enough.": "0515ce6bdd6d.mp3",
  "Seneca wrote that life isn't short. We make it short by wasting it. We guard our money carefully, he said, but give our time away to anyone who asks.": "7ab6d6642df4.mp3",
  "Think about yesterday. Where did your time go that you'd like back?": "ce37436ec43c.mp3",
  "Today, spend your hours like they're worth more than money, because they are.": "54a7ba656164.mp3",
  "Hours disappear into scrolling, worrying, and things we don't really value. Then we say we never have enough time.": "24347262baee.mp3",
  "His advice was simple. Treat your time as your most precious possession, because it's the one thing you can never get back.": "56a72f678cea.mp3",
  "Now think of one thing that truly matters to you. When did you last give it real time?": "893bc7439191.mp3",
  "Choose one block of time today that you'll protect for what matters.": "11a63301656b.mp3",
  "Today's theme: Surfing the urge.": "a4f7e070364b.mp3",
  "Cravings feel like they'll keep building until we give in. But they don't. An urge is more like a wave. It rises, it peaks, and then it falls.": "e712eca2276f.mp3",
  "Think of an urge you often face. A snack, a drink, your phone, a purchase. Picture it arriving.": "e5412ea5b55e.mp3",
  "Today, when an urge rises, ride it for a few breaths before you decide anything.": "c351c9fe77aa.mp3",
  "Therapists call this urge surfing. Instead of fighting the urge or obeying it, you ride it. You notice where it shows up in your body, and you breathe with it while it passes.": "64da9ef4f002.mp3",
  "Every time you surf an urge without acting on it, the next one tends to be a little easier.": "61e94e40cf60.mp3",
  "Notice where you'd feel it in your body. Breathe slowly, and imagine riding the wave up.": "198ce86badf2.mp3",
  "Now picture it cresting, and easing back down. You're still here, and you didn't have to act.": "d8d039738994.mp3",
  "Today's theme: The view from above.": "400834aa9fb7.mp3",
  "Marcus Aurelius often pictured life from high above. Cities, armies and crowds, all small and passing. It helped him hold his worries in proportion.": "aac170bc3385.mp3",
  "Picture yourself sitting right where you are. Now rise slowly above the building.": "3ea6e3ca514f.mp3",
  "Today, when something feels huge, take the view from above.": "3f3210f81bde.mp3",
  "From up there, the email you're dreading, the awkward conversation, the bad day, all shrink to their true size.": "b19c077f7398.mp3",
  "This isn't about feeling unimportant. It's about seeing what's actually big, and what only feels big right now.": "ad46c69c78b3.mp3",
  "Keep rising. See your town, your country, the curve of the earth. Find today's worry from up here.": "c68d898def6d.mp3",
  "From this height, what still seems worth your attention? Come gently back down with that.": "bfcb03c68265.mp3",
  "Today's theme: Name it to tame it.": "ae270eb725a5.mp3",
  "When emotions run high, we often feel them as one big wave. Upset. Stressed. Off. That fog makes them harder to handle.": "56b7c753763b.mp3",
  "Check in with yourself right now. What are you feeling? Try to find the most precise word.": "b1165906b52c.mp3",
  "Today, when a feeling surges, pause, and give it a precise name.": "c293775c5c53.mp3",
  "Studies of emotion suggest that simply putting a feeling into words can turn its volume down. Naming it helps you step back from it.": "5ea1a9aca8a1.mp3",
  "The more precise the word, the better. Not just bad, but disappointed, or lonely, or embarrassed.": "5f142d5d4661.mp3",
  "Is there a second feeling underneath the first? Name that one too.": "65145fd7c436.mp3",
  "Notice what happens to the feelings once they have names.": "6a3e43c4a908.mp3",
  "Today's theme: Rehearsing setbacks.": "75f2e7058df3.mp3",
  "Seneca advised picturing misfortunes before they happen. Not to worry, but to prepare. A blow that's expected, he wrote, lands more lightly.": "c56d6b85a9b5.mp3",
  "Pick one thing you're counting on today. Picture it going wrong, calmly, as if watching a film.": "496bcfc09206.mp3",
  "Today, expect a few bumps, and meet them like you've already practiced.": "530d304af3ad.mp3",
  "Imagine your plans for today going wrong. The meeting runs long. The workout gets cut short. Someone is short with you.": "3df565dc1afe.mp3",
  "When you've already pictured it calmly, you're less likely to be knocked off balance. And you'll notice how often things go fine.": "a7986498df20.mp3",
  "How would you want to respond? See yourself doing that, steady and clear.": "801fd11308a7.mp3",
  "Now notice: even if it goes wrong, you'd be able to handle it.": "8724cfc2a520.mp3",
  "Today's theme: Values as a compass.": "243f1daa53c5.mp3",
  "Goals are like destinations. Once you reach one, it's done. Values are more like a compass direction. You never arrive at being kind, or healthy, or brave. You keep heading that way.": "cb34f0c9189b.mp3",
  "Ask yourself: what do I want to stand for? Pick one or two words that feel true.": "541ab372101b.mp3",
  "Today, let your values point the way, one small step at a time.": "b82f55608866.mp3",
  "Acceptance and commitment therapy puts values at the center. When life gets hard, they tell you which way to turn, even when the destination is far off.": "7c5c973b53f7.mp3",
  "On a day you miss a goal, you can still act on a value in the next five minutes.": "909661fa287d.mp3",
  "Think of a small action today that would move you in that direction.": "a89ffc225f9b.mp3",
  "Picture yourself doing it. Notice how it feels to act from what you care about.": "52e156212314.mp3",
  "Today's theme: Practicing discomfort.": "0003219410c7.mp3",
  "Seneca suggested setting aside a few days to live simply. Plain food, rough clothes, little comfort. Then ask yourself: is this what I was so afraid of?": "122875652abd.mp3",
  "Think of a comfort you lean on most days. How would you feel without it for a day?": "a50f2bd4940a.mp3",
  "Today, do one hard thing on purpose, and notice that you can.": "993efe09b77a.mp3",
  "Choosing small discomforts, like a cold shower, a hard workout, or skipping a treat, teaches you that you can handle more than you think.": "456c8016ab42.mp3",
  "It also makes ordinary comforts feel like gifts again, instead of things you're owed.": "72d9df381e9f.mp3",
  "Choose one small discomfort you could practice this week. Something safe and simple.": "d4d18df32a5f.mp3",
  "Picture yourself doing it, and finding out you're more capable than you thought.": "fc26094be844.mp3",
  "Today's theme: Talking to yourself like a friend.": "af22e81d38f6.mp3",
  "Notice how you speak to yourself after a mistake. Many of us say things we would never say to a friend.": "aa80a0381415.mp3",
  "Bring to mind a recent moment when you were hard on yourself. What did you say?": "13d2abf96abd.mp3",
  "Today, speak to yourself the way you'd speak to someone you care about.": "1b02912fd3ef.mp3",
  "Researchers who study self-compassion describe three parts. Notice that you're struggling. Remember that everyone struggles sometimes. And offer yourself some kindness.": "495752423be9.mp3",
  "This isn't letting yourself off the hook. People who are kind to themselves after a setback are often more likely to try again.": "b668cf1e0668.mp3",
  "Now imagine a good friend in the same spot. What would you say to them?": "9c72b198799e.mp3",
  "Say that to yourself now, slowly, and let it land.": "ec9145fac187.mp3",
  "Today's theme: Only this moment.": "c5399c0f70bb.mp3",
  "Marcus Aurelius noted that the present moment is the only thing we can ever lose, because it's the only thing we really have. The past is gone, and the future isn't here yet.": "c9ed29400839.mp3",
  "Notice a few things right now. The air on your skin, the sounds around you, the weight of your body.": "06b16cbbe7f5.mp3",
  "Today, when your mind runs ahead, come back to the one thing in front of you.": "b01cc7d0211d.mp3",
  "Yet we spend so much of now replaying yesterday, or rehearsing tomorrow.": "b27fe3940a73.mp3",
  "Coming back to this moment, this breath, this task, is where life actually happens.": "04c907fd25ae.mp3",
  "If your mind drifts to the past or the future, gently return to this breath.": "312a1f2ccc6a.mp3",
  "What is the one task in front of you today that deserves your full attention?": "1f00300cd2a2.mp3",
  "Today's theme: Checking the story.": "4707e787b1f9.mp3",
  "When we're anxious, the mind often jumps to the worst case. One mistake becomes, I'll lose my job. A short reply becomes, they're angry with me.": "a98fc89b9aaf.mp3",
  "Think of a worry that's been on your mind. What's the worst case your mind predicts?": "b328c39cb40b.mp3",
  "Today, when your mind jumps to the worst case, ask what's most likely.": "8ac5dbb58e17.mp3",
  "Cognitive behavioral therapy teaches you to treat these thoughts as guesses, and to check them. What's the evidence? What else could explain this? What's most likely?": "78dfb0099e39.mp3",
  "Often the most likely story is far more ordinary than the scary one.": "36418ef70fb1.mp3",
  "Now ask: what's the most likely outcome? And what's the best case?": "a1467b7af0c0.mp3",
  "If a friend had this worry, what would you tell them?": "62306bac96df.mp3",
  "Today's theme: The evening review.": "6e19c411d8d5.mp3",
  "Each night, Seneca would review his day. What bad habit did I resist today? Where did I fall short? Where did I do well?": "9716ecf130af.mp3",
  "Look back over the last day. Where did you act at your best?": "ad9aecf12105.mp3",
  "Tonight, give yourself a few honest minutes to review the day.": "1ee8b0fd702f.mp3",
  "He did it without beating himself up. He saw each day as practice, and the review as a coach going over the game.": "c103e7a8ddc2.mp3",
  "Your evening reflection in this app is built on the same idea. A few honest minutes, every night.": "7442717c659b.mp3",
  "Where did you fall short? Notice it plainly, without judgment.": "401ec17bdf2f.mp3",
  "What's one thing you'll do differently next time?": "4cdcca068419.mp3",
  "Today's theme: Opposite action.": "c2dc68369941.mp3",
  "Every emotion comes with an urge. Fear says avoid. Sadness says withdraw. Anger says attack.": "c01ea9571d25.mp3",
  "Think of a feeling that's been steering you lately. What does it urge you to do?": "12ecade58a62.mp3",
  "Today, when a feeling pushes you somewhere unhelpful, take one small step the other way.": "1ee1a13134ef.mp3",
  "Dialectical behavior therapy has a skill called opposite action. When a feeling's urge won't help you, you gently do the opposite. Feeling low? Go for a walk, or call someone.": "f106695f5613.mp3",
  "Acting opposite to the urge can, over time, change the feeling itself.": "7211fda0b6c6.mp3",
  "Now picture the opposite action. Something small and doable.": "464587a6a327.mp3",
  "Imagine doing it today, and how you might feel afterward.": "1695cca2c418.mp3",
  "Today's theme: The inner citadel.": "4d35d2d86c40.mp3",
  "People seek retreats, Marcus Aurelius wrote, in the country, at the seashore, in the mountains. But you can retreat into yourself any time you like.": "1d489465b138.mp3",
  "Imagine a quiet place inside you. Notice its stillness.": "ba8babd4d3d1.mp3",
  "Today, when things get noisy, take three breaths, and step inside.": "b0a07b734331.mp3",
  "He called a mind free of passion a citadel. Nothing outside can get in unless you let it.": "f95d66d82a7a.mp3",
  "This calm isn't somewhere you travel to. It's somewhere you return to, a few breaths at a time.": "74eef08f6fbc.mp3",
  "Let the noise of the day stay outside the walls for now.": "71af9e6f1023.mp3",
  "Remember that you can come back here in a single breath, anywhere.": "39ebf6c6df2c.mp3",
  "Today's theme: You are the sky.": "d86af1922bbf.mp3",
  "A common image in mindfulness goes like this. Your thoughts and feelings are like weather. Storms, clouds, bright patches. You are the sky.": "0bc633e67df3.mp3",
  "Notice the weather inside you right now. Calm, cloudy, or stormy?": "214be71ae61e.mp3",
  "Today, remember that bad weather passes, and you are the sky that holds it.": "376ea1413ce6.mp3",
  "The weather changes constantly. The sky holds all of it, and isn't harmed by any of it.": "d0a2dda7a3cd.mp3",
  "Acceptance and commitment therapy calls this the noticing self. There is a part of you that can watch your experience, steady, whatever passes through.": "0f6098d516ee.mp3",
  "Now notice the part of you that is noticing. It isn't the weather. It's the sky.": "f12fde96dfb6.mp3",
  "Rest as the sky for a few breaths, and let the weather move.": "766a2b75a78d.mp3",
  "Today's theme: Made for each other.": "3510ff140b8e.mp3",
  "Marcus Aurelius began one morning by reminding himself he'd meet difficult people that day. Then he added: we are made for cooperation, like feet, like hands, like the upper and lower rows of teeth.": "496f511e5b1f.mp3",
  "Think of someone you find difficult. Picture them as a person who also wants to do well.": "01f828bb6375.mp3",
  "Today, treat even difficult people as teammates you haven't figured out yet.": "e238cc765ded.mp3",
  "To work against each other, he wrote, is against nature.": "ace908f9717f.mp3",
  "The people who frustrate you are often struggling too. And you need each other more than it seems.": "1a9f4e9627f4.mp3",
  "What might they be dealing with that you can't see?": "1dc4e3b0496a.mp3",
  "How could you work with them, rather than against them, today?": "9c92d684182c.mp3",
  "Today's theme: Three good things.": "d4d836d7e624.mp3",
  "Our minds are built to notice threats and problems. It kept our ancestors alive. But it means good moments slip past without being noticed.": "32ea62b5958c.mp3",
  "Think of one good thing from the past day, however small. Why did it happen?": "f9837670271f.mp3",
  "Today, keep an eye out for three good things.": "aed36afd5b5b.mp3",
  "One simple practice psychologists have studied is noting three good things each day, and why they happened.": "37c41c519f78.mp3",
  "Over time, it trains your attention to catch what's going right, not just what's going wrong.": "5c53ab33f223.mp3",
  "Now a second. Maybe something someone did for you.": "2daf4a2a25b7.mp3",
  "And a third. Let yourself really feel each one.": "80ad2eb1ac18.mp3",
  "Today's theme: Be one now.": "2de924c393e8.mp3",
  "Marcus Aurelius wrote a blunt note to himself. Stop arguing about what a good person should be. Be one.": "7b305ed51e37.mp3",
  "What's something you've been planning or thinking about, instead of doing?": "d9e12ab41499.mp3",
  "Today, skip the debate, and take the next right step.": "8599c894d85f.mp3",
  "It's easy to read about habits, plan the perfect week, and wait for the right moment. The Stoics cared about action, today, imperfect as it is.": "a113c1f5cb77.mp3",
  "You don't need to be ready. You just need to take the next right step.": "dab79b248201.mp3",
  "What would the next right step be? Make it small enough to do today.": "f64c697477d4.mp3",
  "Picture yourself taking it, without waiting to feel ready.": "07c1ef6e784f.mp3",
  "Today's theme: Action before motivation.": "a67a15bb5842.mp3",
  "We often wait to feel motivated before we act. But motivation tends to come after we start, not before.": "65c9ec5580bd.mp3",
  "Think of something you've been putting off. What's the smallest possible first step?": "ad6ac7a134dd.mp3",
  "Today, don't wait to feel like it. Start tiny.": "4db6008ea4ca.mp3",
  "Therapists who treat low mood often begin with small actions, not big insights. A short walk. A tidy desk. A text to a friend. Doing something small can lift the mood a little.": "aa6b2a8f9873.mp3",
  "So when you don't feel like it, make the first step tiny, and let the feeling catch up.": "6fb5e3ad7df6.mp3",
  "Make it even smaller. Something you could do in two minutes.": "ba5c7ae4298a.mp3",
  "Picture doing just that step, and stopping if you want. Notice whether you'd want to keep going.": "2fb0c2af0829.mp3",
  "Today's theme: Enough.": "ac0967391216.mp3",
  "Seneca wrote that it isn't the person who has too little who is poor, but the one who craves more.": "dc0b3a14dc54.mp3",
  "Think of something you've been wanting to buy or have. Notice the pull of it.": "55b1df6cfed0.mp3",
  "Today, before you reach for more, notice what's already enough.": "44fc979b46d6.mp3",
  "There's always a newer phone, a bigger place, a better deal. Wanting more can feel like ambition, but often it just moves the finish line.": "9d89fcde3e94.mp3",
  "For the Stoics, wealth was wanting less. Noticing what's enough is its own kind of freedom.": "826140c33e57.mp3",
  "Now name, silently, three things you already have that you'd hate to lose.": "99efdf04aa8f.mp3",
  "Ask yourself: what would enough look like here?": "c866f9ed9df2.mp3",
  "Today's theme: Rest is part of the work.": "2e2dd61e726d.mp3",
  "Training doesn't make you stronger on its own. Your body adapts while it recovers. Without rest, effort just piles up as fatigue.": "ff5983b9a50a.mp3",
  "Check in with your energy right now. Are you running on empty, half full, or full?": "4be366bed205.mp3",
  "Today, treat rest as part of the plan, not a reward for finishing it.": "1a29f0b5d3d5.mp3",
  "The same is true for your mind. Focus, patience and willpower all run down, and they come back with real rest. Sleep, a walk, time away from a screen.": "2414d6d60031.mp3",
  "Rest isn't the opposite of discipline. It's part of it.": "1b45db2c06ce.mp3",
  "What kind of rest would help most today? Sleep, quiet, movement, or people?": "a18e9e7fa90d.mp3",
  "Plan one moment of real rest today, and protect it like a meeting.": "5e82502f8052.mp3",
  "Today's theme: Wanting what happens.": "3fa4863a5ade.mp3",
  "Epictetus gave this advice. Don't ask for things to happen as you wish. Wish for them to happen as they do happen, and your life will go smoothly.": "02201f5a7dd2.mp3",
  "Think of something recent that didn't go the way you wanted. Notice the resistance.": "4307c38494c0.mp3",
  "Today, meet what happens with: okay, this is what I'm working with.": "96f783e3f918.mp3",
  "It sounds passive, but it is practical. Most frustration comes from the gap between what we demanded, and what actually happened.": "e41e74c5067a.mp3",
  "When you accept what's already here, your energy is free for the next useful move.": "1213055b731f.mp3",
  "Now try saying silently: this is what happened. I can work with this.": "733b00390c4d.mp3",
  "What's the next useful move, starting from here?": "bed7e9429947.mp3",
  "Today's theme: Beginner's mind.": "9b3a45ca26fe.mp3",
  "In Zen there's an idea called beginner's mind. In the expert's mind there are few possibilities. In the beginner's mind there are many.": "85fe2e5a554c.mp3",
  "Notice your breath as if for the very first time. Its temperature, its rhythm.": "e751921c9c66.mp3",
  "Today, look at one familiar thing as if you'd never seen it before.": "1f570ae41739.mp3",
  "When we've done something a hundred times, we stop really seeing it. Our routine, the people closest to us, our own street, fade into the background.": "a85a874d1b45.mp3",
  "Meeting familiar things with fresh curiosity can make an ordinary day feel new again.": "16abd0ff6607.mp3",
  "Think of a routine you'll do today. How could you do it with fresh eyes?": "ce9990ac56de.mp3",
  "Pick one familiar person you'll see today. What could you be curious about with them?": "9ce249260adc.mp3",
  "Today's theme: The remedy is delay.": "98c77468dd6c.mp3",
  "Seneca wrote a whole book on anger. His best remedy was simple: delay. Anger is strongest in its first moments, and it fades if you wait.": "59932f851218.mp3",
  "Think of a time anger made you act too fast. What happened?": "9a48fc356c3e.mp3",
  "Today, when anger flares, wait before you speak or send anything.": "83f084e40c83.mp3",
  "In those first moments, we say and send things we later regret.": "a7c6166b151a.mp3",
  "A pause isn't weakness. It's giving your better judgment time to arrive.": "52a868999f6f.mp3",
  "Picture the same moment, but this time you pause, and take three breaths.": "dfb7b120dab8.mp3",
  "What would you have said or done after the pause?": "bf8ac4c7376e.mp3",
  "Today's theme: Loving-kindness.": "20240983a93f.mp3",
  "Loving-kindness is an old meditation practice. You silently offer good wishes, first to yourself, then to people close to you, and outward from there.": "1a86109aa535.mp3",
  "Silently say to yourself: may I be well. May I be at ease.": "ac00e55fe4bf.mp3",
  "Today, silently wish someone well, especially someone who tests your patience.": "9981042e841a.mp3",
  "It can feel awkward at first. But it gently trains your mind toward warmth, instead of judgment.": "689fba34d836.mp3",
  "And it's hard to stay irritated with someone while you're wishing them well.": "b59ee39ef567.mp3",
  "Now picture someone you love, and wish the same for them.": "d96efb3349d4.mp3",
  "Now someone you find difficult. May you be well. May you be at ease.": "b9c91b518e72.mp3",
  "Welcome. Sit tall, feet flat on the floor, hands resting on your thighs.": "31f66e435016.mp3",
  "Take a slow breath in, and let it out.": "9f4e6c932495.mp3",
  "Lift your shoulders up toward your ears. Hold for a moment.": "1fadda06b600.mp3",
  "And let them fall.": "cef24279d704.mp3",
  "Now close your eyes and scan down from the top of your head. Your forehead, your jaw. Let them soften.": "7fa31d7f2791.mp3",
  "Your shoulders, your chest, your belly. Let them soften.": "452e731df883.mp3",
  "Your hips, your legs, all the way down to your feet.": "e28ee2274421.mp3",
  "Take one more deep breath in, and let it all go.": "7b40b66f0543.mp3",
  "Notice how your body feels now.": "5584b83b3ad9.mp3",
  "Gently drop your right ear toward your right shoulder. Breathe into the left side of your neck.": "fc7d660cd255.mp3",
  "Slowly bring your head through the middle, and over to the left.": "fe04a64fff07.mp3",
  "And back to center.": "d56880988ebe.mp3",
  "Reach your right arm overhead and lean gently to the left. Feel the stretch along your side.": "bb1abe42d963.mp3",
  "Come back up, and reach the other arm over to the right.": "590a22cf8b1d.mp3",
  "Place your left hand on your right knee and twist gently to the right. Look over your shoulder.": "9f8fa4a932da.mp3",
  "Come back to center, and twist to the other side.": "62507e35a1b9.mp3",
  "Now fold forward slowly, letting your arms and head hang heavy.": "23d71a4505d3.mp3",
  "Roll up one vertebra at a time, head last.": "fa7f81b77191.mp3",
  "Welcome. Get comfortable, and let your body be heavy.": "989dea9216ba.mp3",
  "There's nothing left to do today.": "872b15c929c1.mp3",
  "We'll breathe in for four, hold for seven, and breathe out slowly for eight.": "5292b65f41f5.mp3",
  "Keep breathing slowly. Drift off whenever you are ready.": "c99006a87512.mp3",
  "Let your jaw loosen. Let your eyes soften.": "5d78750035aa.mp3",
  "Feel the bed holding you up. You don't have to hold anything.": "f896abc40da2.mp3",
  "If thoughts about today come up, picture setting each one down on a shelf. They'll be there tomorrow.": "2e8e45dd7718.mp3",
  "Now just breathe naturally, and count each out-breath, from ten down to one.": "e33b386d495f.mp3",
  "Breathe in": "38be7ab90061.mp3",
  "And slowly out": "6c8452bd60d5.mp3",
  "In": "49b308a3550c.mp3",
  "A little more": "99a053bdb3ec.mp3",
  "Long sigh out": "6643e75aa4df.mp3",
  "Hold": "fc657db3d66b.mp3",
  "Out": "7d54b3f8a4c2.mp3",
  "And out, slowly": "c85cb8f20e44.mp3"
 }
